{
 "lead": "A ductile metal in a wing fitting sees two or three normal stresses plus shear. It yields when the shear, or the distortion energy, reaches the value measured in a plain tension test. Two criteria bracket the answer: maximum shear stress (Tresca), the conservative one, and distortion energy (von Mises), the usual design choice. Octahedral shear is von Mises in another dress. Stress engineers in design offices use these to turn a multiaxial state into one equivalent stress that is compared with the yield strength σ<sub>Y</sub>.",
 "prereq": "You need <a href=\"#failure-theories-maximum-principal-stress-and-strain\">Chapter 3.6</a> for principal stresses and strains.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Tresca and von Mises",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Tresca says yield starts when the largest shear anywhere reaches σ<sub>Y</sub>/2. In principal stresses σ<sub>1</sub> ≥ σ<sub>2</sub> ≥ σ<sub>3</sub>:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "σ<sub>eq,T</sub> = max(|σ<sub>1</sub> − σ<sub>2</sub>|, |σ<sub>2</sub> − σ<sub>3</sub>|, |σ<sub>3</sub> − σ<sub>1</sub>|) ≤ σ<sub>Y</sub>",
     "where": "σ<sub>1</sub>, σ<sub>2</sub>, σ<sub>3</sub> are principal stresses in MPa; σ<sub>Y</sub> is the tensile yield strength in MPa."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In plane stress one principal stress is zero. If the other two have opposite signs, σ<sub>eq,T</sub> is their difference. If they have the same sign, it is the larger magnitude alone. Taking the difference in both cases is the commonest error."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "von Mises says yield starts when distortion energy — the strain energy that changes shape, not volume — reaches its tension-test value. Hydrostatic pressure changes volume only and cannot yield a ductile metal. In principal stresses:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "σ<sub>eq,M</sub> = √[(σ<sub>1</sub> − σ<sub>2</sub>)² + (σ<sub>2</sub> − σ<sub>3</sub>)² + (σ<sub>3</sub> − σ<sub>1</sub>)²] / √2",
     "where": "σ<sub>1</sub>, σ<sub>2</sub>, σ<sub>3</sub> are principal stresses in MPa; the result is in MPa."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For plane stress with σ<sub>3</sub> = 0 this becomes σ<sub>eq,M</sub> = √(σ<sub>1</sub>² − σ<sub>1</sub>σ<sub>2</sub> + σ<sub>2</sub>²). In stress components:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "σ<sub>eq,M</sub> = √(σ<sub>x</sub>² − σ<sub>x</sub>σ<sub>y</sub> + σ<sub>y</sub>² + 3τ<sub>xy</sub>²)",
     "where": "σ<sub>x</sub>, σ<sub>y</sub> are normal stresses and τ<sub>xy</sub> is shear stress, all in MPa."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The 3 on τ<sub>xy</sub>² is not optional."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Octahedral shear and comparison",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The octahedral plane has a normal making equal angles with the three principal axes. Its shear stress is"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "τ<sub>oct</sub> = (1/3)√[(σ<sub>1</sub> − σ<sub>2</sub>)² + (σ<sub>2</sub> − σ<sub>3</sub>)² + (σ<sub>3</sub> − σ<sub>1</sub>)²]",
     "where": "τ<sub>oct</sub> is the octahedral shear stress in MPa; σ<sub>1</sub>, σ<sub>2</sub>, σ<sub>3</sub> are principal stresses in MPa."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Compare the square roots: σ<sub>eq,M</sub> = (3/√2)τ<sub>oct</sub>. So yielding when τ<sub>oct</sub> = √2 σ<sub>Y</sub>/3 is exactly von Mises, not a third independent theory. The octahedral shear carries the whole distortion; the mean stress (σ<sub>1</sub>+σ<sub>2</sub>+σ<sub>3</sub>)/3 is the normal stress on the octahedral plane; it changes volume only and leaves τ<sub>oct</sub> unchanged."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Tresca gives the larger equivalent stress, so it is conservative. The von Mises value is √(3/2) times the root-mean-square of the three principal differences, so it still cannot exceed the largest difference. Use Tresca for a quick hand check, von Mises for design and finite element work. Only a few universities teach the octahedral form here."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
